% Test cases for the therapeutic shoes policy (therapeutic_shoes.le):
% synthetic claims, one or two per criterion of Policy Article A52501 and
% LCD L33369 and per payment condition the Article adds, each stating only
% what its record shows. The one finding ("deformity requires custom molded
% shoe") is recorded as the treating practitioner's or the Council's; a
% finding nobody made leaves the answer conditional. The queries: whether
% the claim is payable (the first query, so "stage" reads its checklist),
% whether it is reasonable and necessary, and what minimal change would
% make it payable.

the target language is: prolog.

the knowledge base therapeutic shoes cases includes these resources:
    therapeutic_shoes.

the knowledge base therapeutic shoes cases includes:

query pay is:
    which claim is payable.

query rn is:
    which claim is reasonable and necessary.

query stage is:
    the query fails at which section.

query flip is:
    which minimal change to the scenario makes it the case that
        claim 1 is payable.

% A complete file: a pair of depth shoes (claim 1) with a pair of inserts
% (claim 2) for a beneficiary with type 2 diabetes and peripheral neuropathy
% with callus formation — the certification, the visits, the supplier's
% evaluations, the orders and the deliveries all in the record.
scenario depth_shoes_with_inserts_complete is:
    claim 1 is for the depth shoes.
    the HCPCS code of the depth shoes is "A5500".
    the depth shoes is furnished to Ann.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-01.
    claim 2 is for the inserts.
    the HCPCS code of the inserts is "A5512".
    the inserts is an accessory of the depth shoes.
    the inserts is furnished to Ann.
    the date of service of claim 2 is 2026-03-01.
    the residence of Ann is private residence.
    the diagnosis code of Ann is "E11.42".
    the medical record of Ann documents peripheral neuropathy with callus formation.
    the certifying physician of Ann is doctor of medicine.
    the certifying physician treats Ann under a comprehensive plan of care for diabetes.
    the certifying physician certified that Ann needs diabetic shoes.
    the certification statement for Ann is signed by the certifying physician.
    the certification statement for Ann is dated 2026-02-10.
    the certifying physician had an in-person visit with Ann addressing diabetes management on 2026-01-15.
    the medical record of the certifying physician documents management of the diabetes of Ann.
    the prescribing practitioner for the depth shoes is podiatrist.
    the prescribing practitioner for the inserts is podiatrist.
    the prescribing practitioner had an in-person visit with Ann on 2026-02-05.
    the supplier conducted an in-person evaluation of the feet of Ann with measurements before selecting the depth shoes.
    the supplier conducted an in-person evaluation of the feet of Ann with measurements before selecting the inserts.
    the count of pairs of shoes furnished to Ann in the calendar year of claim 1 is 1.
    the count of pairs of inserts furnished to Ann in the calendar year of claim 2 is 1.
    the order for the depth shoes is dated 2026-02-10.
    the order for the depth shoes is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the depth shoes includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the depth shoes includes order date.
    the order for the depth shoes includes description of the item.
    the order for the depth shoes includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the depth shoes includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the depth shoes was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-12.
    the depth shoes was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the depth shoes.
    the supplier assessed the fit of the depth shoes at delivery with Ann wearing it.
    the order for the inserts is dated 2026-02-10.
    the order for the inserts is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the inserts includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the inserts includes order date.
    the order for the inserts includes description of the item.
    the order for the inserts includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the inserts includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the inserts was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-12.
    the inserts was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the inserts.
    the supplier assessed the fit of the inserts at delivery with Ann wearing it.
    pay expects answers ["claim 1 is payable", "claim 2 is payable"].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 2 is reasonable and necessary"].
    stage expects answers [].

% A diagnosis code that is not diabetes (I10, essential hypertension):
% criterion 1 fails.
scenario non_diabetic_diagnosis is:
    claim 1 is for the depth shoes.
    the HCPCS code of the depth shoes is "A5500".
    the depth shoes is furnished to Ben.
    the residence of Ben is private residence.
    the diagnosis code of Ben is "I10".
    the medical record of Ben documents poor circulation.
    the certifying physician of Ben is doctor of medicine.
    the certifying physician treats Ben under a comprehensive plan of care for diabetes.
    the certifying physician certified that Ben needs diabetic shoes.
    the prescribing practitioner for the depth shoes is podiatrist.
    the supplier conducted an in-person evaluation of the feet of Ben with measurements before selecting the depth shoes.
    the count of pairs of shoes furnished to Ben in the calendar year of claim 1 is 1.
    rn expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at question"].

