    the count of pairs of shoes furnished to Cy in the calendar year of claim 1 is 1.
    rn expects answers [].

% A fourth pair of inserts in the calendar year with depth shoes: over the
% limit of three.
scenario fourth_pair_of_inserts is:
    claim 1 is for the inserts.
    the HCPCS code of the inserts is "A5512".
    the inserts is an accessory of the depth shoes.
    the HCPCS code of the depth shoes is "A5500".
    the depth shoes is furnished to Dee.
    the inserts is furnished to Dee.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-11-02.
    the diagnosis code of Dee is "E10.40".
    the medical record of Dee documents history of foot ulceration.
    the certifying physician of Dee is doctor of medicine.
    the certifying physician treats Dee under a comprehensive plan of care for diabetes.
    the certifying physician certified that Dee needs diabetic shoes.
    the prescribing practitioner for the inserts is podiatrist.
    the supplier conducted an in-person evaluation of the feet of Dee with measurements before selecting the inserts.
    the count of pairs of inserts furnished to Dee in the calendar year of claim 1 is 4.
    rn expects answers [].

% Custom molded shoes (A5501) for a foot deformity the treating practitioner
% finds no depth shoe could accommodate; the second pair of inserts of the
% year is within the limit of two additional pairs, the third is not.
scenario custom_molded_with_deformity is:
    claim 1 is for the custom shoes.
    the HCPCS code of the custom shoes is "A5501".
    the custom shoes is furnished to Eve.
    claim 2 is for the second inserts.
    the HCPCS code of the second inserts is "A5513".
    the second inserts is an accessory of the custom shoes.
    the second inserts is furnished to Eve.
    claim 3 is for the third inserts.
    the HCPCS code of the third inserts is "A5513".
    the third inserts is an accessory of the custom shoes.
    the third inserts is furnished to Eve.
    the diagnosis code of Eve is "E11.42".
    the medical record of Eve documents foot deformity.
    the finding deformity requires custom molded shoe about Eve is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "a rigid claw toe deformity of both feet".
    the certifying physician of Eve is doctor of medicine.
    the certifying physician treats Eve under a comprehensive plan of care for diabetes.
    the certifying physician certified that Eve needs diabetic shoes.
    the prescribing practitioner for the custom shoes is podiatrist.
    the prescribing practitioner for the second inserts is podiatrist.
    the prescribing practitioner for the third inserts is podiatrist.
    the supplier conducted an in-person evaluation of the feet of Eve with measurements before selecting the custom shoes.
    the supplier conducted an in-person evaluation of the feet of Eve with measurements before selecting the second inserts.
    the supplier conducted an in-person evaluation of the feet of Eve with measurements before selecting the third inserts.
    the count of pairs of shoes furnished to Eve in the calendar year of claim 1 is 1.
    the count of pairs of inserts furnished to Eve in the calendar year of claim 2 is 2.
    the count of pairs of inserts furnished to Eve in the calendar year of claim 3 is 3.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 2 is reasonable and necessary"].

% The same custom molded shoes, but the Council finds the record does not
% show a deformity a depth shoe could not accommodate.
scenario custom_molded_deformity_unsupported is:
    claim 1 is for the custom shoes.
    the HCPCS code of the custom shoes is "A5501".
    the custom shoes is furnished to Fay.
    the diagnosis code of Fay is "E11.42".
    the medical record of Fay documents foot deformity.
    the finding deformity requires custom molded shoe about Fay is unsupported, according to the Council, because "the supplier's records describe a mild bunion and no reason a depth shoe would not accommodate it".
    the certifying physician of Fay is doctor of medicine.
    the certifying physician treats Fay under a comprehensive plan of care for diabetes.
    the certifying physician certified that Fay needs diabetic shoes.
    the prescribing practitioner for the custom shoes is podiatrist.
    the supplier conducted an in-person evaluation of the feet of Fay with measurements before selecting the custom shoes.
    the count of pairs of shoes furnished to Fay in the calendar year of claim 1 is 1.
    rn expects answers [].

% Custom molded shoes about which nobody has said whether a depth shoe
% would do: the answer is conditional on that finding.
scenario custom_molded_no_finding is:
    claim 1 is for the custom shoes.
    the HCPCS code of the custom shoes is "A5501".
    the custom shoes is furnished to Gus.
    the diagnosis code of Gus is "E11.42".
    the medical record of Gus documents foot deformity.
    the certifying physician of Gus is doctor of medicine.
    the certifying physician treats Gus under a comprehensive plan of care for diabetes.
    the certifying physician certified that Gus needs diabetic shoes.
    the prescribing practitioner for the custom shoes is podiatrist.
    the supplier conducted an in-person evaluation of the feet of Gus with measurements before selecting the custom shoes.
    the count of pairs of shoes furnished to Gus in the calendar year of claim 1 is 1.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"] and unknowns ["the finding deformity requires custom molded shoe about Gus is established"].

% A rigid rocker bottom (A5503) on depth shoes: a modification substitutes
% for an insert, and this is the second of the year.
scenario modification_rocker_bottom is:
    claim 1 is for the rocker bottom.
    the HCPCS code of the rocker bottom is "A5503".
    the rocker bottom is an accessory of the depth shoes.
    the HCPCS code of the depth shoes is "A5500".
    the depth shoes is furnished to Hal.
    the rocker bottom is furnished to Hal.
    the diagnosis code of Hal is "E11.621".
    the medical record of Hal documents previous foot amputation.
    the certifying physician of Hal is doctor of medicine.
    the certifying physician treats Hal under a comprehensive plan of care for diabetes.
    the certifying physician certified that Hal needs diabetic shoes.
    the prescribing practitioner for the rocker bottom is nurse practitioner.
    the supplier conducted an in-person evaluation of the feet of Hal with measurements before selecting the rocker bottom.
    the count of pairs of inserts furnished to Hal in the calendar year of claim 1 is 2.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].

% Inserts dispensed without shoes: covered when the supplier verifies in
% writing that the beneficiary has appropriate footwear (claim 1), not
% otherwise (claim 2, another supplier's inserts with no verification).
scenario inserts_dispensed_independently is:
    claim 1 is for the inserts.
    the HCPCS code of the inserts is "A5514".
    the inserts is an accessory of the depth shoes.
    the inserts is furnished to Ida.
    claim 2 is for the other inserts.
    the HCPCS code of the other inserts is "A5514".
    the other inserts is an accessory of the depth shoes.
    the other inserts is furnished to Ida.
    the HCPCS code of the depth shoes is "A5500".
    the supplier verified in writing that Ida has appropriate footwear for the inserts.
    the diagnosis code of Ida is "E11.42".
    the medical record of Ida documents preulcerative calluses.
    the certifying physician of Ida is doctor of medicine.
    the certifying physician treats Ida under a comprehensive plan of care for diabetes.
    the certifying physician certified that Ida needs diabetic shoes.
    the prescribing practitioner for the inserts is physician assistant.
    the prescribing practitioner for the other inserts is physician assistant.
    the supplier conducted an in-person evaluation of the feet of Ida with measurements before selecting the inserts.
    the supplier conducted an in-person evaluation of the feet of Ida with measurements before selecting the other inserts.
    the count of pairs of inserts furnished to Ida in the calendar year of claim 1 is 1.
    the count of pairs of inserts furnished to Ida in the calendar year of claim 2 is 2.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].

